Lichen Planus
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Pruritic purple polygonal papules, Wickham striae, Flexural distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-027
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of intractable pruritus and rapidly spreading, painful purple skin plaques leading to severe sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a patient experiencing an acute, severe flare of intensely itchy, painful skin lesions that had completely prevented sleep for 48 hours. The intense pruritus was out of proportion to the rash and causing acute psychological distress. Urgent out-of-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose the eruption, rule out a severe cutaneous adverse drug reaction, and prescribe potent systemic and topical therapies to break the intractable itch cycle before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My wrists and ankles are covered in these purple bumps that itch so deeply I feel like I'm going insane. Nothing I put on them helps."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, aggressive eruption of intensely pruritic, purple, planar papules that began 5 days ago and has rapidly worsened over the last 48 hours. The predominant distribution is on the flexor surfaces of the bilateral wrists and ankles. The patient notes an intensely pruritic nature that feels "deep" and is out of proportion to the objective size of the rash, leading to severe insomnia and emotional exhaustion. They report the Koebner phenomenon, with new linear lesions localizing to areas where the skin was recently scratched. The patient denies any new medications, recent illnesses, or known Hepatitis C exposure.
Functional Status: Independent, but functionally impaired by severe fatigue and continuous pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute psychosocial distress secondary to intractable itch and sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Agitated, visibly exhausted, continuously rubbing the wrists and ankles rather than scratching them directly.
Physical Exam: The flexor surfaces of the wrists, distal forearms, and anterior ankles display multiple clusters of firm, violaceous, polygonal, planar papules and small plaques. Close inspection with a dermatoscope reveals a classic fine white reticulated network on the surface of the papules (Wickham striae). Several linear arrangements of papules are present along excoriation lines (Koebnerization). Examination of the oral cavity reveals delicate, lacy white plaques on the bilateral buccal mucosa without active painful ulceration. The nails and scalp are currently clear. There is no generalized erythema, blistering, or systemic lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Patient has multiple over-the-counter anti-itch creams visible, none providing relief.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Flare of Lichen Planus (Cutaneous and Oral) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Psoriasis (Guttate/Plaque) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly violaceous colour, flexural rather than extensor distribution, presence of Wickham striae, and lack of thick silvery scale.

Scabies - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of the finger webs, absence of visible burrows, and the distinct polygonal, planar morphology of the papules.

Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a herald patch, non-truncal distribution (heavily acral), and the intensely violaceous, severely pruritic nature of the lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe flare of Lichen Planus, characterized by the classic 6 P's (pruritic, purple, planar, polygonal papules/plaques) and oral mucosal involvement. Triage accurately flagged the intractable pruritus and severe sleep deprivation as an acute crisis requiring intervention. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was critical to differentiate this immune-mediated condition from an acute infectious or allergic crisis (like scabies or contact dermatitis) which require vastly different treatments. By initiating a short, potent burst of systemic corticosteroids alongside ultra-high-potency topical steroids, the severe inflammatory cascade was immediately targeted, breaking the agonizing itch cycle and preventing a likely emergency department presentation for acute distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 5-7 days to rapidly suppress the systemic immune response. Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment BD strictly to the active lesions. Recommended a sedating antihistamine (Promethazine 25mg) at night to aid sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying this strong steroid ointment specifically to the purple bumps and strictly avoiding scratching the skin, you will stop the trauma that signals your body to create new lesions, allowing the rash to heal faster. By taking the oral steroid exactly as prescribed each morning, you will forcefully shut down the overactive immune cells causing the deep itch, letting you finally sleep through the night.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ordered baseline fasting blood tests including Hepatitis C serology for follow-up.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess response, review blood results, and plan the steroid taper.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the oral white patches become severely painful open ulcers preventing you from drinking fluids, or if you develop a severe, widespread blistering rash, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Lichen planus is a chronic immune condition that can take months to fully resolve. The initial treatment aims to control the intense symptoms.
Clinical Course: The extreme itching should significantly decrease within 48 hours on the oral steroids. The purple bumps will eventually flatten but typically leave behind flat, brown marks (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation) that take many months to fade.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical review if you become completely unable to eat or drink due to mouth pain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has mistakenly attacked your skin and the lining of your mouth, causing these extremely itchy purple bumps. Because the inflammation is deep, standard anti-itch creams fail; the oral steroids and strong ointment are required to physically switch off the immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Do not scrub the skin in the shower. Apply the ointment sparingly only to the active bumps.

